# 4. The Problem We Are Solving

Traditional AI security focuses heavily on:

- prompt injection
- data leakage
- unauthorized access
- malicious outputs
- model abuse

Denial of Wallet focuses on a different failure mode:

> **An autonomous AI agent can become economically dangerous even when the model itself is functioning correctly.**

Examples:

### Request Flood

An agent enters a loop and sends hundreds of requests.

```text
Normal:
10 requests/min

Attack:
300 requests/min
```

The model may still produce perfectly valid answers.

The problem is the bill.

### Token Explosion

An agent repeatedly requests extremely long outputs.

```text
Normal:
2K tokens/request

Attack:
50K tokens/request
```

Again, the model is behaving normally.

The wallet is not.

### Tool Loop

An autonomous agent repeatedly calls tools and then asks the model to reason about the result.

```text
LLM
 ↓
Tool
 ↓
LLM
 ↓
Tool
 ↓
LLM
 ↓
Tool
 ↓
...
```

The loop can create rapidly increasing inference costs.

### Model Escalation

An agent moves from a cheap model to an expensive model without an appropriate reason.

```text
cheap model
    ↓
medium model
    ↓
expensive model
    ↓
frontier model
```

Denial of Wallet treats this as an economic security signal.

